  <h1>Does responding to Google reviews help SEO?</h1>
  <div class="meta">Updated 9 October 2026. Every Google quote below was checked against Google's help pages on that date.</div>

  <div class="highlight"><p>Short answer: Google does not say replies are a ranking factor. It says more reviews and positive ratings can help local ranking, and that replies show you value feedback and &ldquo;can help your business stand out.&rdquo; Anything beyond that, including the claims that reply rate or keywords in replies move rankings, is unconfirmed. What is measured is the effect on customers, and through them on review volume and ratings.</p></div>

  <h2>What Google's documentation says, word for word</h2>
  <p>These are the statements about reviews, replies and ranking on the three Google Business Profile Help pages that cover them. Each quote is copied from the page linked.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table class="data stack">
    <thead><tr><th>Google's words</th><th>What it does and does not say</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td data-label="Google's words">&ldquo;Local results are mainly based on relevance, distance, and popularity.&rdquo; <span class="src">(<a href="https://support.google.com/business/answer/7091" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tips to improve your local ranking</a>)</span></td><td data-label="What it does and does not say">The three headline factors. Replies are not named among them.</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Google's words">&ldquo;This factor's also based on info like how many websites link to your business and how many reviews you have. More reviews and positive ratings can help your business's local ranking.&rdquo; <span class="src">(same page, under Prominence)</span></td><td data-label="What it does and does not say">Review count and rating are documented ranking inputs. Replies are not mentioned here.</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Google's words">&ldquo;When you reply to customer reviews, it shows that you value their feedback. Positive reviews and helpful replies can help your business stand out.&rdquo; <span class="src">(same page, under Respond to reviews)</span></td><td data-label="What it does and does not say">Replying sits in Google's list of ways to improve local ranking, but the benefit described is standing out to people, not a ranking signal.</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Google's words">&ldquo;There's no way to request or pay for a better local ranking on Google. We do our best to keep the search algorithm details confidential to make the ranking system as fair as possible for everyone.&rdquo; <span class="src">(same page)</span></td><td data-label="What it does and does not say">Google does not publish weights, so nobody outside Google can state the effect of replies as fact.</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Google's words">&ldquo;Helpful and positive replies to reviews can show that you're responsive to your customers.&rdquo; <span class="src">(<a href="https://support.google.com/business/answer/3474050" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Manage customer reviews</a>)</span></td><td data-label="What it does and does not say">Again framed around customers.</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Google's words">&ldquo;Value all reviews: Honest and balanced reviews can help potential customers decide. A mix of positive and negative feedback often feels more trustworthy. As best practice, reply to reviews and share your perspective.&rdquo; <span class="src">(<a href="https://support.google.com/business/answer/3474122" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tips to get more reviews</a>)</span></td><td data-label="What it does and does not say">Google recommends replying as best practice. No ranking claim.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>Claims you will read elsewhere, checked</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table class="data stack">
    <thead><tr><th>Claim</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td data-label="Claim">More reviews and better ratings help local ranking</td><td data-label="Evidence">Stated by Google (quoted above).</td><td data-label="Status"><span class="verdict v-doc">Documented</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Claim">Google measures your reply rate and ranks you on it</td><td data-label="Evidence">Not stated by Google. We found no published test that isolates reply rate.</td><td data-label="Status"><span class="verdict v-unk">Unconfirmed</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Claim">Keywords in your replies help you rank for them</td><td data-label="Evidence">Not stated by Google. The closest public test we found, a 2023 Sterling Sky case study, added keywords to reviews (not owner replies) for one business; rankings did not improve.</td><td data-label="Status"><span class="verdict v-unk">Unconfirmed</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Claim">Replies count as fresh content that Google rewards</td><td data-label="Evidence">Not stated by Google.</td><td data-label="Status"><span class="verdict v-unk">Unconfirmed</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Claim">Fast replies rank higher</td><td data-label="Evidence">Google asks for timely replies but makes no ranking claim about speed.</td><td data-label="Status"><span class="verdict v-unk">Unconfirmed</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Claim">Businesses that reply get more reviews and higher ratings</td><td data-label="Evidence">Measured for hotels (Proserpio and Zervas, below). We found no equivalent study of Google reviews.</td><td data-label="Status"><span class="verdict v-part">Measured elsewhere</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>The one route from replies to ranking that fits Google's documentation</h2>
  <p>Google says more reviews and positive ratings can help ranking. A peer-reviewed study found that replying is followed by more reviews and slightly better ratings. Put together, replies could help ranking indirectly, through the reviews they encourage. That chain is an inference, and the study was not about Google:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The study.</strong> Davide Proserpio and Georgios Zervas, &ldquo;Online Reputation Management: Estimating the Impact of Management Responses on Consumer Reviews&rdquo;, <em>Marketing Science</em> 36(5), 2017. Using differences in how often the same hotels reply on different review platforms, they found &ldquo;a 0.12-star increase in ratings and a 12% increase in review volume for responding hotels.&rdquo;</li>
    <li><strong>A detail worth knowing.</strong> The same study found that once hotels started replying, they received fewer but longer negative reviews. The authors' explanation is that unhappy customers became less likely to post short, unsupported complaints when they expected the hotel to answer.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What replies measurably change: the people reading them</h2>
  <p>BrightLocal's Local Consumer Review Survey 2026 asked 1,002 US adults about reviews. These are stated preferences rather than observed behaviour, but they are recent and specific to replies:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table class="data">
    <thead><tr><th>Finding</th><th class="num">Share of consumers</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Expect business owners to respond to reviews</td><td class="num">89%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Likely to use a business that responds to all of its reviews</td><td class="num">80%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Put off by generic or templated replies</td><td class="num">50%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Unlikely to use a business that never replies</td><td class="num">42%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Count an owner's response among the most important review factors</td><td class="num">37%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Expect a reply within a week</td><td class="num">81%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>Google's own guidance points the same way from the other side: it says a mix of positive and negative reviews &ldquo;often feels more trustworthy&rdquo;, and that &ldquo;Providing constructive replies and following up on concerns can show that you care and may even encourage the customer to update their review.&rdquo;</p>

  <h2>What can count against you</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Replies are moderated.</strong> Google checks every reply against its content policies before posting it, usually within 10 minutes and sometimes up to 30 days. A reply that is not approved does not appear.</li>
    <li><strong>Incentives break the rules.</strong> Google does not allow merchants to offer payment, discounts or free goods or services in exchange for a review, or for revising or removing a negative one. Reviews posted because of an incentive are removed.</li>
    <li><strong>Keyword-stuffed replies are written for the wrong reader.</strong> Whatever its ranking effect, a reply listing your services and city does not answer the reviewer, and the people deciding whether to visit read it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For what to write instead, see <a href="/blog/how-to-respond-to-google-reviews">how to respond to Google reviews</a> and the <a href="/blog/negative-review-response-templates">negative review templates</a>.</p>

  <h2>So, should you bother?</h2>
  <p>Yes, for reasons that do not depend on the ranking question. Customers read replies and say they prefer businesses that answer every review. In the one large study that measured it, replying was followed by more reviews and slightly higher ratings, and those two things are what Google does say can help local ranking. If replies have a direct ranking effect as well, Google has not said so.</p>

